---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { REVIEWER, AUTHOR } from '../consts';
const trail = [{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Editorial Policy', href: '/editorial-policy/' }];
---
<Page
  title="Editorial Policy"
  eyebrow="How this site works"
  description="Who writes Going Eagle's content, who reviews it, what qualifications those reviewers hold, and the rules we hold ourselves to."
  intro="Credibility comes from being transparent about who writes the content, who reviews it, and what those people are actually qualified to say. Here is exactly how Going Eagle works."
  trail={trail}
>
  <h2>Who writes, who reviews</h2>
  <p>
    Articles on Going Eagle are researched and written by the <strong>{AUTHOR.name}</strong>.
    Golf content is then reviewed before publication by
    <a href={REVIEWER.url}>{REVIEWER.name}</a>, a golf coach with six years of coaching
    experience. Mike checks the advice for accuracy, practical usefulness and soundness,
    gives feedback where it is needed, and approves the final version before it goes live.
  </p>
  <p>
    We keep those two roles separate and label them separately on every article. Mike is a
    reviewer, not a bylined author, and we will never present him as having written something
    he did not write.
  </p>

  <h2>What the review badge means</h2>
  <p>
    When you see <em>“Reviewed by Mike Evans, Golf Coach”</em> on an article, it means that
    specific article was read and approved by Mike before publication. Pages without that badge
    have not been through coach review yet — usually because they are reference or
    administrative pages rather than golf advice.
  </p>

  <h2>Rules we hold ourselves to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No invented credentials.</strong> We publish the qualifications our reviewers
      actually hold and nothing more. We do not publish a handicap index for Mike because we
      do not have a verified figure to substantiate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No stock or AI-generated portraits of real people.</strong> Where we do not yet
      have a genuine photograph, we use a plain monogram and say so.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No fabricated testing.</strong> If we have not physically tested a product, the
      page says the recommendation is research-based. See <a href="/gear/how-we-test/">how we test</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No invented statistics.</strong> Where we cite a figure we say where it came from.
      Where a reliable figure does not exist, we say that instead of estimating one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Commission never changes a ranking.</strong> If the product paying us less is the
      better product, it is listed first. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Illustrations are labelled.</strong> Diagrams and illustrative images are our own.
      We never present an illustration as a photograph of a real product or event.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Keeping content current</h2>
  <p>
    Golf advice ages at different speeds. A definition of a stroke index will be true in ten
    years; a list of the best launch monitors will be wrong within one. Every page on this site
    is assigned a review schedule when it is written:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Type of content</th><th scope="col">Reviewed every</th><th scope="col">Examples</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Fast-moving</th><td>3 months</td><td>Product recommendations, prices, rankings, rules changes</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Moderate</th><td>6 months</td><td>Comparisons, buying guides, anything tied to a specific tool</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Stable</th><td>12 months</td><td>Definitions, technique fundamentals, background explanations</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    We change the “last updated” date on an article only when the content genuinely changed.
    Refreshing a date to look current, without touching the substance, is a practice we will
    not use.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If something here is wrong, we want to know. Tell us through the
    <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> and we will correct it. Substantive corrections are
    noted on the page itself rather than quietly edited away.
  </p>
</Page>
